.TH SYSMON.CONF 5 "21 June 2000" "Version 0.83" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
sysmon.conf \- sysmond(man) configuration file
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I sysmon.conf
file is the main configuration file for the
.BR sysmond (man)
which monitors systems and services on various machines connected to a
network.

Every rule consists of at least three fields, and at most nine.

Lines starting with a hash mark (``#'') and empty lines are ignored.

This release of
.B sysmond
is able to understand an extended syntax.  A rule may have a replacement
performed on it, if a variable is declared earlier in the configuration
file.

.SH GLOBAL SETTINGS
There are various global settings that
.BR sysmond 
can have configured.  Currently there is some
inconsistency with some of the settings, but we expect to make
them more standard in a newer version of 
.BR sysmond .

.SS Values that can be configured with ``set''

pmesg sender from subject upcolor downcolor recentcolor

These variables are configured in the following format

.IP
.nf
set pmesg = "value"
set sender = "value"
set from = "value"
set subject = "value"
set upcolor = "value"
set downcolor = "value"
set recentcolor = "value"
.fi
.LP

.SS Generic Variables
The set command allows the creation of generic variables which can
be automatically replaced further down in a configuration file.  This
can be used to seperate routers from servers, while still providing
an easy way to update all the devices being monitored with a new "contact".
The example that follows, all cases of $oncall will be replaced with
oncall@example.com

Example:
.IP
.nf
set oncall = "oncall@example.com"

router.example.com ping "main router" $oncall

.fi
.LP

.SS Values that can be configured with ``config''

queuetime include showupalso errorsto maxqueued replyto noheartbeat nologconnects
pageinterval logging dnslog dnsexpire statusfile statustempdir nosubject
numfailures minnumfailures flaptime html refresh pidfile output drop_privileges
disable_icmp trap_port snmp-trap authkey savestate

.SS queuetime
The ``config queuetime'' directive is used to specify the number of
seconds between the completion of the last test of the service
and the start of the next test of the service.  This time is in seconds,
and the default is 60.

.SS include
The ``config include'' directive is used to specify the path to another
file that should be included at that point in the current file.  This
is useful if you have software that generates some of your configuration
files, and you wish to have a per-router configuration file, while
maintaining your current dependencies.  Other uses could be having an
oncall database that is generated to allow automatic changing of shifts.

.SS showupalso
The ``config showupalso'' directive allows all hosts (both those as
being monitored as up and down) and services being monitored to appear in
the html or text status file that is created by sysmond.  This does
not affect the display of the curses client.

.SS errorsto
The ``config errorsto'' directive inserts an Errors-to: header in any
e-mail that sysmond generates to a contact.  This is useful if the
address that sysmond is sending from is not a real account, to allow
bounces or page-not-sent messages to be collected in a central location.

.SS maxqueued
The ``config maxqueued'' directive takes an integer argument that
specifies the number of simultaneous checks that may be performed at
once.  Large networks may experience problems with polling if sysmond
receives a large number of icmp responses at once because many hosts
are being pinged at once, or if the operating system or daemon has
a restricted number of filedescriptors available.  The default is 100.

.SS replyto
The ``config replyto'' directive inserts a Reply-to: header in any
e-mail that sysmond generates to a contact.  This is useful to have
users that reply to a message about an outage reach a support person
at an organization.

.SS noheartbeat
The ``config noheartbeat'' directive disables the default behavior of
sysmond to send a registration packet to our registration server.  This
information is collected to determine the hardware platforms and
operating systems being used for monitoring so we can concentrate
testing and development on these systems.  Disabling this feature will
cause no adverse affect to monitoring, as the packet is sent blindly
once on startup.

.SS pageinterval
The ``config pageinterval'' directive allows you to configure a reminder
interval that a host is still down.  This is an integer representing 
the number of minutes since the last e-mail message to a contact
about the outage.  Once that timer has been reached, another message is
sent.  This is useful for people who sleep through their pagers.

.SS logging 
The ``config logging'' directive allows you to specify a syslog facility
to be logged to.  These options vary slightly from one operating system
to the next.  Sysmon supports logging to the following facilities:
.IP
.nf
kern user mail daemon auth syslog lpr news uucp cron authpriv
local0-local7 none
.fi
.LP
The default is daemon.

.SS dnslog
The ``config dnslog'' directive allows you to configure the logging
interval of the internal dns cache statistics.  This number is
an integer representing the number of seconds between logging intervals.
The default is 900 seconds (15 minutes).

.SS dnsexpire
The ``config dnsexpire'' directive allows you to configure the internal
time-to-live of dns entries cached.  This number is an integer representing
the number of seconds an element should be allowed to stay in the cache.
The default is 1500 seconds (25 minutes).

.SS statusfile
The ``config statusfile'' directive takes two arguments.  The first is the
specification of the file, be it "text" or "html".  The second argument is
the path to the file that gets written.  The default is to not write a
status file.

.SS statustempdir
The ``config statustempdir'' directive specifies a directory where
sysmond should write temporary status files before renaming them to
their final location.  This is particularly useful when sysmond has
dropped privileges using the ``drop_privileges'' directive, as it
allows the daemon to write status files to a directory owned by the
unprivileged user (typically ``nobody'').  The directory must exist
and be writable by the user sysmond runs as.  If not specified,
temporary files are created in the same directory as the final status
file (which requires write permissions on that directory).

Example:
.IP
.nf
config statustempdir /var/run/sysmon;
.fi
.LP

After setting this directive, ensure the directory is created and has
appropriate permissions:
.IP
.nf
mkdir -p /var/run/sysmon
chown nobody:nobody /var/run/sysmon
chmod 755 /var/run/sysmon
.fi
.LP

.SS nosubject
The ``config nosubject'' directive specifies that there should be no
subject in the messages sent to the contact address.  This is necessary
to not consume characters for some e-mail to pager gateways such that
the full message gets delivered.

.SS numfailures
The ``config numfailures'' directive takes an integer argument and
specifies the number of times that a site must be monitored as
down before a message is sent to the contact address.  The default is
4.

.SS minnumfailures
The ``config minnumfailures'' directive takes an integer argument and
specifies the minimum number of consecutive failures required before a
host appears in the status file as down. This reduces false alarms from
transient network issues. A host will only show as down when both
numfailures and minnumfailures thresholds are met. The default is 1.
Example: config minnumfailures 2;

.SS flaptime
The ``config flaptime'' directive takes an integer argument in minutes
and specifies how long to keep a host marked as "up" (green) after it
recovers from a down state. This prevents the visual "flapping" effect
where hosts rapidly alternate between up and down in the status display.
During the flap time period, the host appears stable even if subsequent
checks fail, reducing alert fatigue. The default is 0 (disabled).
Example: config flaptime 5;

.SS sleeptime
The ``config sleeptime'' option is now obsolete.  See the queuetime
configuration option.

.SS html refresh
Supply an integer argument in seconds (default 60) to put in the
Refresh pseudo-header of the HTML status page.

.SS date
Supply a string which may contain formatting codes from strftime(3)
(default "%x %X"), or the shorthand words "ISO" ("%F %T") or "DEC"
("%T %d-%b-%Y").

.SS output
The ``config output json'' directive enables JSON output mode. Sysmond
writes monitoring data in JSON format to the specified file path. The
JSON output includes detailed information about all monitored objects,
their states, SNMP trap alerts, packet loss statistics, RTT/jitter
measurements, and wakeup check results. Updated whenever state changes occur.
Example: config output json "/var/log/sysmon/status.json";

.SS drop_privileges
The ``config drop_privileges'' directive enables privilege dropping for
enhanced security. When enabled, sysmond switches to a non-privileged
user after initialization. ICMP operations are performed via the setuid
sysmon-ping-helper binary. Cannot be used with disable_icmp.
Example: config drop_privileges;

.SS disable_icmp
The ``config disable_icmp'' directive disables all ICMP (ping and pingv6)
monitoring. This prevents the daemon from opening raw sockets and allows
it to run entirely without privileges. Note that this disables all ping
and pingv6 tests in the configuration.
Example: config disable_icmp;

.SS trap_port
The ``config trap_port'' directive specifies the UDP port for receiving
SNMP trap alerts. Default is 162 (standard SNMP trap port). Requires
root privileges or CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE capability to bind ports below 1024.
Example: config trap_port 162;

.SS snmp-trap
The ``config snmp-trap'' directive enables SNMP trap monitoring globally.
When enabled, sysmond opens a UDP socket on the trap_port (default 162)
to receive SNMP trap PDUs. Individual objects can then use the trap_alert
directive to enable trap monitoring for specific hosts. Traps are matched
to objects by source IP address. Requires trap_port to be configured.
Example: config snmp-trap;

.SS nologconnects
The ``config nologconnects'' directive disables logging of client connections
to the sysmon daemon. When enabled, connection messages from clients are
suppressed from the logs, reducing log verbosity in environments with
frequent client connections or monitoring systems that poll regularly.
Useful for reducing log noise while maintaining operational logging.
Example: config nologconnects;

.SS authkey
The ``config authkey'' directive sets an authentication key required for
privileged client commands. Clients must send "AUTH <key>" to authenticate
before performing certain operations. The daemon responds with "333 Good
Authentication" on success or "444" on failure. This provides basic access
control for administrative operations via the client protocol.
Example: config authkey "secret_password_here";

.SS savestate
The ``config savestate'' directive specifies a file path where sysmond
will periodically save the complete monitoring state in XML format. This
is primarily useful for debugging and understanding the internal state of
all monitored objects. The file contains detailed information about each
object including check counts, timestamps, and current status. Not required
for normal operation.
Example: config savestate "/var/log/sysmon/state.xml";

.SH OBJECT OPTIONS
Once you have declared an object, additional configuration options are
available beyond those documented in earlier versions:

.SS min_pings
The ``min_pings'' directive specifies the minimum number of successful
ping responses required to consider the host UP. Used for packet loss
monitoring. Must be less than or equal to send_pings. Default is 1.
Example: min_pings 4;

.SS send_pings
The ``send_pings'' directive specifies the number of ICMP echo requests
to send during each check interval. Used for packet loss monitoring.
Higher values provide more accurate loss statistics. Default is 5.
Example: send_pings 5;

.SS packet_loss_threshold
The ``packet_loss_threshold'' directive specifies the packet loss
percentage threshold (0.0-100.0). If packet loss exceeds this value,
an alert is triggered even if the minimum number of pings succeeded.
Default is 0.0 (disabled).
Example: packet_loss_threshold 25.0;

.SS rtt_threshold
The ``rtt_threshold'' directive specifies the round-trip time threshold
in milliseconds. If average RTT exceeds this value, an alert is triggered.
Used for latency monitoring.
Example: rtt_threshold 100;

.SS jitter_threshold
The ``jitter_threshold'' directive specifies the jitter threshold in
milliseconds. Jitter is the variation in RTT between consecutive packets.
If jitter exceeds this value, an alert is triggered. Used for monitoring
connection stability.
Example: jitter_threshold 50;

.SS wakeup_check
The ``wakeup_check'' directive enables proactive wakeup monitoring.
When enabled, sysmond attempts to wake unreachable hosts using
Wake-on-LAN before declaring them down. Useful for monitoring hosts
that may be in power-saving modes.
Example: wakeup_check;

.SS wakeup_retries
The ``wakeup_retries'' directive specifies the number of Wake-on-LAN
attempts before declaring the host permanently down. Only used when
wakeup_check is enabled. Default is 3.
Example: wakeup_retries 3;

.SS wakeup_check_interval
The ``wakeup_check_interval'' directive specifies the time to wait in
seconds between wakeup attempts. Allows time for the host to complete
the boot process. Only used when wakeup_check is enabled. Default is 300.
Example: wakeup_check_interval 300;

.SS trap_alert
The ``trap_alert'' directive enables SNMP trap alert monitoring for this
object. When enabled, sysmond listens for SNMP traps from this host and
triggers alerts based on trap severity and content. Traps are matched to
objects by source IP address.
Example: trap_alert;

.SS matched_host
The ``matched_host'' directive associates a trap monitoring object with
a specific host object. Used to link SNMP trap alerts with the
corresponding monitored host. Allows trap-triggered state changes to
affect the associated host's status.
Example: matched_host "router1";

.SS snmp-type
The ``snmp-type'' directive specifies the type of SNMP check to perform.
Valid types are: high (alert if value exceeds threshold), low (alert if
value is below threshold), range (alert if outside range), exact (alert
if not matching exact value), rate (alert if counter rate exceeds threshold),
and uptime (monitor system uptime). Must be used with corresponding
snmp-high, snmp-low, snmp-exact, or snmp-rate directives.
Example: snmp-type "high";

.SS snmp-high
The ``snmp-high'' directive sets the upper threshold for SNMP monitoring.
When snmp-type is "high", alerts are triggered if the polled SNMP value
exceeds this threshold. When snmp-type is "range", this sets the upper
bound of the acceptable range. Value is an unsigned integer.
Example: snmp-high 1000000;

.SS snmp-low
The ``snmp-low'' directive sets the lower threshold for SNMP monitoring.
When snmp-type is "low", alerts are triggered if the polled SNMP value
falls below this threshold. When snmp-type is "range", this sets the lower
bound of the acceptable range. Value is an unsigned integer.
Example: snmp-low 100;

.SS snmp-exact
The ``snmp-exact'' directive specifies the exact value expected from an
SNMP query. When snmp-type is "exact", alerts are triggered if the polled
value does not match this exact value. Useful for monitoring specific
states or counters that should remain constant. Value is an unsigned integer.
Example: snmp-exact 1;

.SS snmp-rate
The ``snmp-rate'' directive sets the maximum acceptable rate of change for
an SNMP counter. When snmp-type is "rate", sysmond calculates the rate of
change between polls and alerts if it exceeds this threshold. Use with
snmp-octets to convert bytes/sec to bits/sec. Value is rate per second.
Example: snmp-rate 100000;

.SS snmp-octets
The ``snmp-octets'' directive modifies SNMP rate calculations to convert
from bytes to bits by multiplying by 8. Only affects snmp-type "rate" checks.
Useful for monitoring network interface bandwidth where counters are in
bytes but you want to alert on bits per second (e.g., Mbps).
Example: snmp-octets;

.SS pageinterval
The ``pageinterval'' directive allows per-object override of the global
page interval setting. This is an integer representing the number of
minutes between reminder alerts for a down host. Critical hosts can be
configured with shorter intervals for more frequent alerts, while less
important hosts can have longer intervals. If not specified, the global
pageinterval setting is used.
Example: pageinterval 30;

